---
/**
 * foodcraft / CredentialsBM — "How we keep standards." (Tahanan Cafe).
 *
 * ROLE: the design's CREDENTIALS band. Unlike the previous template in this
 * family this one is NOT a boxed rail — it is a SPLIT: an eyebrow and a
 * display headline held in a narrow left column (.72fr), and to the right of
 * them (1.28fr, 50px of gap) a two-up list of credential cells whose only
 * ornament is a HAIRLINE ACROSS THE TOP OF EVERY CELL. The grid is drawn with
 * borders, never with gaps — the cells sit flush at gap:0 and the 26px of
 * right padding on each cell IS the gutter, so the two top rules of a row meet
 * and read as one continuous line across the measure. Each cell opens with an
 * italic walnut serif numeral, then an 11px tracked-caps title, then a 13.5px
 * sentence. It is the café's standing on record, which is exactly why every
 * word of it is owner-typed and none of it is drawn here.
 *
 * DESIGN FIDELITY — every value below is lifted from the CREDENTIALS element of
 * "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html" and mapped hex → token (R1). No literal hex:
 *   split       minmax(0,.72fr) minmax(0,1.28fr), gap 50px, align-items:start
 *   eyebrow     10px / .34em / 700 / #7A4E2D on a 1px #E0D3C6 underline with
 *               9px of padding ..... EXACTLY the wrapper's .kick. Not restated
 *               (R-brief: restating the eyebrow loses the signature underline),
 *               so var(--accent) + var(--line-2) arrive from the primitive.
 *   headline    clamp(25px,3.1vw,40px) / 1.16 / #241B14, margin .26em 0 0, with
 *               an italic #7A4E2D <em> ...... var(--ink) + var(--accent) via the
 *               wrapper's .h2 and its global `:where(.h2 em)`. Size, leading and
 *               margin ARE restated — see DECISION 2, this band's headline is
 *               deliberately smaller than the wrapper's shared .h2 scale.
 *   cells grid  repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)), gap 0 ... var-free, verbatim
 *   cell        padding 22px 26px 22px 0, border-top 1px #EDE6DF
 *               ................................... var(--line)
 *   numeral     Libre Caslon Text ITALIC 18px #7A4E2D, margin-bottom 10px, and
 *               NO line-height — it computes to `normal` in the design document
 *               ................................... the wrapper's .fig, which
 *               ALREADY carries var(--disp), weight 400, tabular lining figures
 *               and var(--accent) here; the italic, the 18px AND the leading are
 *               local (.fig ships line-height:1, see DECISION 4)
 *   title       11px / .2em / 700 / uppercase / #241B14, margin 0 0 8px, and no
 *               line-height — `normal` again, not the h1-h4 reset's 1.14
 *               ................................... var(--sans) + var(--ink)
 *   body        13.5px / 1.75 / #5C5147, margin 0, max-width 40ch
 *               ................................... var(--ink-soft)
 * The accent is the one token the design itself exposes as configurable (its
 * own data-props seeds a picker at #7A4E2D), and therefore the one token an
 * admin scheme is welcome to move.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — all four are declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts under section id 'credentials'; nothing here is a
 * dead hook:
 *   credentials.tag ................ the eyebrow
 *   credentials.headline ........... the h2, fed with set:html so an owner can
 *                                    place the design's italic walnut emphasis
 *                                    anywhere inside their OWN sentence
 *   credentials.items.${i}.title ... the tracked-caps cell title
 *   credentials.items.${i}.body .... the sentence under it. CANONICAL key is
 *                                    `body` — that is what the schema declares
 *                                    and what the sidebar writes ({title, body}).
 *                                    The reader below still ACCEPTS desc /
 *                                    detail / description as input for sites
 *                                    saved before the schema settled, but the
 *                                    hook it emits always spells `body`.
 *
 * DERIVED, and therefore carrying NO data-field:
 *   · the numeral (01, 02, 03…) is built from the DISPLAY index, so the list
 *     always counts without gaps however many blank rows were dropped, and an
 *     edit can never be misdirected into a counter (R6). The design's own seed
 *     data is zero-padded to two digits ('01'…'04'); padStart reproduces that.
 *     aria-hidden — it is ornament, and the title beside it is the content (R9).
 *   · the cells' track count: two tracks at two rows and above, and exactly one
 *     at a single row, so one lone credential fills its column instead of
 *     hanging half-width off its left edge.
 *   · the split's track count: one track when there is no head to hold, so the
 *     cells never land in the narrow .72fr column with 1.28fr of white beside
 *     them.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. content.credentials has no derived
 * fallback whatsoever: astro-builder passes `c.credentials ?? undefined` and
 * derive-content-defaults never invents a credential, so this band exists only
 * where an owner put something on record. Consequences, all deliberate:
 *   · with no filled row the WHOLE <section> is omitted — no eyebrow, no
 *     headline, no rules, no padding (R5: the gate counts the ROWS, never the
 *     tag or the headline, because keepHead can backfill those two from a
 *     derived block and a gate counting them would ship a heading over nothing).
 *   · title and body are guarded independently and OUTSIDE their tags, so a
 *     half-filled row prints only what was typed — never an empty <h3> holding
 *     open 8px of margin under the numeral, and never a hairline over a row
 *     that is not there.
 *   · those guards are trim-AWARE only because the reader coerces and trims
 *     once, before anything else touches the value: a title of "   " is truthy,
 *     and guarded raw it would ride through the row filter on its sibling's
 *     body and print exactly that empty heading.
 *   · the head guard sits outside its column div too, so an owner who typed
 *     neither field gets no empty box beside the list.
 *   · the design's demo strings ("Local beans", "Roast date on the bag",
 *     "Barista trained", "Sanitation certified") are the DESIGNER filling in
 *     their own template. A café that never claimed SCA training must not have
 *     this page claim it for them, and a kitchen that is not city-certified
 *     must not be captioned as one.
 *   · NO seal, shield, ribbon, stamp, tick or badge of any kind, and no logo
 *     strip. A drawn badge is a visual claim of certification the owner never
 *     made; the design draws none either. The numeral and the hairline are the
 *     whole ornament.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of it reads as an oversight:
 *   · NO star row. R2's rating rule is real but has nothing to bind to here: a
 *     credential carries no numeric rating field in the schema, and five drawn
 *     stars would be a fabricated claim. The design draws stars in
 *     TESTIMONIALS, not in CREDENTIALS.
 *   · NO onPage() anchor-remap helper and no id. 'credentials' is not one of
 *     PageBM's ids (menu about gallery visit faq main), nothing on the page
 *     links here, and — decisively — this section emits NO href of any kind:
 *     the design gives CREDENTIALS no link and no button. A helper here would
 *     be an unused const with nothing to remap. R7 exists to stop an anchor
 *     pointing at an id this page has not got, not to require a helper where
 *     there is no anchor. CredentialsBL records the same decision. If a later
 *     revision adds an action to this band, copy the helper from HeaderBM /
 *     ServicesBM wholesale rather than hand-rolling a shorter one, and use
 *     PAGE_ANCHORS = menu about gallery visit faq main with the family's
 *     ANCHOR_ALIAS (location|contact→visit, services|rooms→menu, host|why→
 *     about, work→gallery, book→main).
 *   · NO `band-dark`. The design keeps CREDENTIALS on the white page ground;
 *     the closing CTA band is the only dark ground in the whole document.
 *   · NO .plate / .ph and no role="img": the design gives this band no
 *     photograph, so there is no image slot to offer and no accessible name to
 *     invent for one (R9).
 *   · NO button of any kind, so none of the forbidden theme-engine labels
 *     (.btn-primary .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi) appear here,
 *     and `.hero` is not on this <section> (R4).
 *   · NO data-block marker: editorBridge only needs one where two hook prefixes
 *     share a <section>. Everything here is credentials.*, so the Blocks toggle
 *     already resolves by hook weight.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE QUESTION:
 *   1. VERTICAL RHYTHM AND MEASURE ARE THE WRAPPER'S. The design writes
 *      `padding:84px 30px 0` and `max-width:1160px` on this section; PageBM's
 *      shared `section { padding: clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0 }` already tops out at
 *      exactly 84px and `.wrap`'s min(1160px,92vw) reproduces the measure. So
 *      this file adds no section padding of its own — changing it here alone
 *      would desynchronise this band from its fourteen siblings.
 *   2. THE HEADLINE SCALE IS RESTATED, AND THE WRAPPER'S 760 PASS IS
 *      DELIBERATELY SUPERSEDED. This band's h2 is the design's SMALL headline —
 *      clamp(25px,3.1vw,40px)/1.16 — where the wrapper's shared .h2 is
 *      clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px)/1.14. A local class beats the wrapper's
 *      `html[data-page="BM"] :where(.h2)` on specificity at every width,
 *      INCLUDING inside its `@media (max-width:760px)` block, and that is
 *      required rather than incidental: the wrapper's mobile pass resolves to
 *      38px at 759px while this band's own clamp is already floored at 25px by
 *      761px, so letting the shared rule win would make the headline JUMP FROM
 *      25px TO 38px as the viewport NARROWS past 760 — the exact grow-as-it-
 *      shrinks bug the audit found on the previous template. The design's clamp
 *      is monotonic across every breakpoint here (40px ≥1290 · 27.9px @900 ·
 *      25px @760 · 25px @520 · 25px @360) and is therefore never overridden in
 *      a media query below.
 *   3. THE WRAPPER'S 24ch MEASURE CAP IS LEFT IN PLACE, not released. The
 *      design's CREDENTIALS h2 carries no max-width, but it does not need one:
 *      its .72fr column is ~400px wide at the 1160 measure, narrower than 24ch
 *      of 40px Libre Caslon, so the cap never bites at desktop and the design
 *      draws exactly as authored. Between 900px (where the split collapses) and
 *      760px (where the wrapper releases the cap itself) the cap is the only
 *      thing holding a sane measure on a full-width headline, so removing it
 *      would be a regression with no fidelity gain.
 *   4. BOTH LOCAL LEADINGS ARE `normal`, WHICH IS THE DESIGN'S OWN VALUE — not a
 *      chosen number. Neither the numeral nor the title declares a line-height
 *      in the design, and the design's body sets none either, so both compute to
 *      `normal`. This wrapper is not so kind: it pins line-height 1 on .fig and
 *      1.14 on the h1–h4 reset, so left inherited the numeral's 18px box would
 *      come out ~6px shorter than drawn (printing the design's 10px gap tight)
 *      and a two-line title would sit tighter than the design ever set it.
 *      Restating `normal` reproduces the drawing exactly and needs no judgement
 *      call; HowBM restates it on the same two elements for the same reason.
 *      Nothing else about either is guessed: face, weight, size, tracking,
 *      transform, colour and margin are all the design's.
 *   5. A TRAILING HALF-ROW IS LEFT AS THE DESIGN DRAWS IT. The hairline is
 *      per-cell (`border-top` on every repeated cell, verbatim from the design's
 *      sc-for), so an odd number of credentials ends with a rule that runs half
 *      the measure. That is the drawing the design specifies rather than
 *      something to tidy into a full-width rule, and it reads as a ruled list
 *      that stops, not as a grid with a hole in it.
 *   6. THE DIVIDER IS A BORDER, never a box-shadow. Grid items paint in
 *      order-modified document order, each atomically (CSS Grid §6.2), so a
 *      hairline cast as a shadow from the cell above lands under the next
 *      cell's own box. A shadow-drawn divider renders nowhere.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped <style> block, no is:global. The headline is injected with
 * set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach an <em> inside it; its italic walnut
 * styling comes from PageBM's global `:where(.h2 em)` and needs nothing local,
 * which is why no :global() rule appears below. (No literal end-style-tag is
 * written inside any comment here or in the stylesheet — the parser would end
 * the sheet there and silently truncate the file.)
 *
 * CONTRAST, on this design's white ground and using PageBM's recorded figures:
 * ink/paper 14.9 (the titles) · accent/paper 7.06 (the eyebrow and the
 * numerals) · ink-soft/paper 7.29 (the 13.5px sentence). Every text colour in
 * this band clears AA on its own; --muted, the one value in this palette that
 * sits nearest the line, is not used here at all.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBM hands it to all fifteen sections
// uniformly. This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never
// destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// The schema declares 'credentials.items' with fallbackPaths ['credentials'],
// so the stored value is legitimately EITHER {tag, headline, items:[…]} or a
// bare array. Both shapes are read.
const cred = content.credentials || {};

// COERCE, THEN TRIM, ONCE AT THE READER — the house rule the BM siblings share.
// Two reasons, both real:
//   · a value that arrives as a NUMBER — an owner who typed `2024` into a
//     credential title, or an older AI save — has no .trim(), so calling it in
//     the row filter below would throw and take the whole build with it.
//   · trimming here is what makes every guard downstream trim-AWARE (see R2 in
//     the block above).
// Trimming is not supplying content: the owner's words are untouched, only the
// whitespace around them.
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '';

// The eyebrow is credentials.tag ONLY. The design's "Credentials" literal is
// the designer filling in their own template; it is not this file's to type.
const tag = str(cred.tag).trim();
const headline = str(cred.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  title?: unknown; label?: unknown;
  body?: unknown; desc?: unknown; detail?: unknown; description?: unknown;
};
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(cred.items)
  ? cred.items
  : (Array.isArray(cred) ? cred : []);

const titleOf = (it: Item): string => (str(it.title).trim() || str(it.label).trim());
// Reads the aliases as INPUT (older saves, and normalizeBlock's desc copy) but
// the data-field emitted below always spells the canonical `body`. `||` rather
// than `??` deliberately: normalizeBlock copies body into desc whenever desc is
// merely absent, so a row saved with body:'' and a real desc must fall through
// rather than stop on the empty string.
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string =>
  (str(it.body).trim() || str(it.desc).trim() || str(it.detail).trim() || str(it.description).trim());

// R6 — a row an owner added but never filled would otherwise draw a hairline, a
// numeral and 22px of padding over nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace counts
// as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index, so every data-field still writes to
// the real array slot: removing credential 2 must never redirect an edit into
// credential 3. The numeral below uses the DISPLAY index instead, because it is
// decorative and must keep counting 01, 02, 03 without gaps.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ ...it, i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(titleOf(r) || bodyOf(r)));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
// Two tracks is the design; one lone credential takes one track rather than
// hanging half-width off the left edge of its column.
const cellCols = Math.min(rows.length, 2);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="crdbm">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class:list={['crdbm-split', !hasHead && 'crdbm-split--solo']}>
        {hasHead && (
          <div class="crdbm-head reveal">
            {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="credentials.tag">{tag}</span>}
            {headline && (
              <h2 class="h2 crdbm-h" data-field="credentials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
            )}
          </div>
        )}
        <div class:list={['crdbm-cells', 'reveal', `crdbm-cells--${cellCols}`]} data-delay="1">
          {rows.map((it, k: number) => (
            <div class="crdbm-cell">
              {/* DISPLAY index — the list reads 01, 02, 03 however many blank
                  rows were dropped. No data-field: it is a counter, never
                  stored. ONE literal aria-hidden and never a conditional one
                  beside it (R9). */}
              <span class="fig crdbm-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              {titleOf(it) && (
                <h3 class="crdbm-t" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.title`}>{titleOf(it)}</h3>
              )}
              {bodyOf(it) && (
                <p class="crdbm-p" data-field={`credentials.items.${it.i}.body`}>{bodyOf(it)}</p>
              )}
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The section keeps PageBM's shared vertical padding and the white page
     ground. No band-dark: the closing CTA band is the only dark ground in this
     design. See DECISION 1. */
  .crdbm { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE SPLIT, verbatim: a narrow head column and a wider list column, 50px
     apart, top-aligned so the eyebrow's underline sits level with the first
     cell's hairline. */
  .crdbm-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, .72fr) minmax(0, 1.28fr);
    gap: 50px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* An owner who typed neither tag nor headline gets no empty head column, so
     the list must not be left sitting in the narrow .72fr track. */
  .crdbm-split--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  .crdbm-head { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow IS the wrapper's .kick — nothing about the design's span is
     restated, so this band cannot fork from the other fourteen sections and
     cannot lose the signature underline. The one thing ADDED is a wrap rule the
     wrapper never sets: .kick is an inline-block carrying .34em of tracking, so
     one long unbroken owner word is unusually wide for its character count and
     would push the document sideways at 360px (R8). */
  .crdbm-head .kick { max-width: 100%; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* This band's headline is the design's SMALL h2 — see DECISION 2 for why the
     size and leading are restated here rather than inherited, and why this rule
     must keep winning inside the wrapper's 760 pass. The 24ch measure cap is
     LEFT IN PLACE (DECISION 3). Face, colour and the italic walnut <em> still
     come from the shared .h2 and its global em rule. */
  .crdbm-h {
    font-size: clamp(25px, 3.1vw, 40px);
    line-height: 1.16;
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* THE LIST. Two tracks, gap ZERO — the grid is drawn with the cells' own top
     hairlines, and the 26px of right padding on each cell is what stands in for
     a gutter. With gap:0 the two rules of a row meet and read as one line
     across the measure. */
  .crdbm-cells { display: grid; gap: 0; min-width: 0; }
  .crdbm-cells--1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .crdbm-cells--2 { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }

  /* The design's own declaration, verbatim: the hairline is on the TOP of every
     repeated cell, so it rules above the first row too. See DECISION 5 for the
     trailing half-row and DECISION 6 for why this is a border, not a shadow. */
  .crdbm-cell {
    padding: 22px 26px 22px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* Libre Caslon Text italic 18px walnut, then 10px of air. .fig already
     supplies the face, weight 400, tabular lining figures and — unlike the
     previous template's .fig — var(--accent). Three things are local: the
     italic, the size, and the LEADING. The design states no line-height on this
     div, so it computes to `normal`, while .fig ships line-height:1 — an 18px
     box where the design draws ~24px, which would print the 10px gap under the
     numeral about 6px tighter than drawn. Written `.crdbm-n.fig` so it clears
     the wrapper's (0,1,1) :where(.fig) with room to spare, whatever the source
     order. See DECISION 4. */
  .crdbm-n.fig {
    display: block;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: normal;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  /* PageBM sets :where(h1,h2,h3,h4) to Libre Caslon Text 400 at line-height
     1.14, so face, weight, size, tracking, transform, colour and margin are all
     restated to reach the design's Karla tracked caps. The leading is restated
     too, and to the design's own value: its h3 declares none and neither does
     its body, so it computes to `normal` — DECISION 4. Qualified with the cell
     class so it beats that (0,1,1) reset with room to spare. */
  .crdbm-cell .crdbm-t {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 11px;
    line-height: normal;
    letter-spacing: .2em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* PageBM's `:where(p){margin:0 0 1em}` is (0,1,1); qualifying with the cell
     class puts this clear of it so the design's zero margin actually lands. */
  .crdbm-cell .crdbm-p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    max-width: 40ch;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── 900 · R8. The split collapses: a ~400px head column beside a two-up list
        of 11px tracked caps stops working well before this, and the design's
        50px gap is a horizontal measure — stacked it becomes a vertical one, so
        it drops to the 34px this design uses between a headline and the block
        under it. The list itself stays two-up: full width, its tracks are now
        wider than they were at desktop. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .crdbm-split { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 34px; }
  }

  /* ── 520 · one column, and nothing may push the page sideways: the eyebrow,
        the headline, the titles and the bodies all break anywhere, and the list
        is a grid of minmax(0,1fr) tracks, so a long unbroken word cannot widen
        it past the wrap. The headline is NOT re-clamped here — its own clamp is
        already at its 25px floor by 760px and re-stating a larger floor is what
        makes a headline grow as the viewport narrows (DECISION 2). */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .crdbm-split { gap: 28px; }
    .crdbm-cells--2 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    /* The 26px of right padding was the GUTTER between two tracks; with one
       track there is no gutter to hold, so it goes and the hairline runs the
       full measure. That is the ONLY thing that moves: the design's 22px of
       vertical padding is kept verbatim, because stacking does not change what
       it buys — two-up or one-up, adjacent rows are always 22px + 22px apart. */
    .crdbm-cell { padding: 22px 0; }
  }
</style>
